ec319a75 (613 1793263632088 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f368") (src . "code-suggest: an ECMAScript scan that keeps every live reading\n\nJavaScript and TypeScript files fell back to one block at their first\n\"/\", backtick or (in TypeScript) \"<\", outside strings and comments: any\ndivision, regex literal, template literal or generic made a file\nunscannable, so most real files could be neither split nor cut at an\neditor's symbols. A \"/\" after a token that can end an expression is a\ndivision or a regex, and which one is not decided by the token's\nspelling (automatic semicolons, contextual keywords, TypeScript's postfix\n\"!\" and type-argument \">\"). So the scan now keeps every reading that can\nstill be right and lets the text disprove the rest.\n\nA reading is the complete scanner state: the pair stack with template and\ninterpolation frames, the literal it is in (string, template, regex, its\nclass, comment), what the last significant token can be followed by, an\noperand the statement still owes (it survives comments and line ends), a\nstring's line continuation, and whether the line has had code yet.\nStates are interned, so readings at the same place in the same state\nmerge, and their histories of line judgments merge into a shared graph.\nAt a \"/\" a reading forks into a regex and a division unless the token\nbefore it cannot end an expression. A reading dies where the text\ndisproves it: a regex or a quote open at a line end, a closer that does\nnot match, anything open at the end of the file. A line is at the top\nlevel only if every reading that survives the whole file says so; past\n32 readings the file falls back as before. The decision for each line is\nthe table scan's own, extracted once and shared by both scans.\n\nThe file falls back for what the scan does not follow: a lone carriage\nreturn, U+2028 or U+2029 anywhere; in JavaScript, Annex B's HTML-like\ncomments, and JSX, recognised by its closing forms (\"<\" then \"/\", or \"/\"\nthen \">\", whitespace and line ends between allowed) anywhere in the text.\nIn TypeScript a run of \">\" and a postfix \"!\" can end an expression and\n`void` is also a type. `default` is read like `return` before a \"/\".\n\n- code-suggest.sc: the ecmascript scan, the JSX and line-terminator\n  pre-passes, and the per-line decision shared with the table scan.\n- languages.sc: javascript and typescript use the ecmascript scan.\n- Tests: ecmascript-scan (safety rows for literals hidden by a wrong\n  reading, the ambiguous \"/\" cases, templates, the strict continuation,\n  the cap, the line terminators, JSX); code-suggest1's fallback rows for\n  JavaScript moved to it; import-symbols' comment row for JavaScript now\n  refused (the owed operand); rows-baseline.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 505))))
